!id: lWHDLySwFu
!front: Derive why \( \int \frac{1}{x} \, dx = \ln |x| + C \) for \( x < 0 \) or \( x > 0 \).
!back:
Let:

- \( F(x) = \ln |x| \)
- \( x \neq 0 \)

To prove: \( F'(x) = \frac{1}{x} \).

- If \( x > 0 \), then \( F(x) = \ln x \), so \[ F'(x) = \frac{1}{x}. \]
- If \( x < 0 \), then \( F(x) = \ln(-x) \), so the chain rule gives \[ F'(x) = \frac{-1}{-x} = \frac{1}{x}. \]
- Therefore \( \ln |x| \) is an antiderivative of \( \frac{1}{x} \) on any interval where \( x < 0 \) or \( x > 0 \).
~~~
